{
  "id": "L-003",
  "title": "Continuity",
  "atom_ids": ["A-03"],
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 1,
      "content": [{ "type": "text", "text": "Continuity" }]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Continuity is the property that lets you draw a graph without lifting your pen. The precise version ties together three things at a single point: the function has a value there, it has a limit there, and the two agree."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "definition",
      "term": "Continuous at a point",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "A function "
        },
        { "type": "math", "source": "f" },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": " is continuous at "
        },
        { "type": "math", "source": "x = a" },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": " when all three hold: "
        },
        { "type": "math", "source": "f(a)" },
        { "type": "text", "text": " is defined, " },
        { "type": "math", "source": "\\lim_{x \\to a} f(x)" },
        { "type": "text", "text": " exists, and " },
        {
          "type": "math",
          "source": "\\lim_{x \\to a} f(x) = f(a)"
        },
        { "type": "text", "text": "." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The failing cases each break a different condition. The function in L-001 failed the first: "
        },
        { "type": "math", "source": "g(1)" },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": " is undefined. A step function fails the second: the two one-sided limits disagree, so no two-sided limit exists. And a function with a removable hole that has been filled with the wrong value fails the third."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "src": "figures/jump-discontinuity.png",
      "atom_id": "A-03",
      "caption": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "A jump discontinuity at "
        },
        { "type": "math", "source": "x = 2" },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": ": the left-hand limit is 1, the right-hand limit is 3, and no single two-sided limit exists."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "worked-example",
      "title": "Where is this function continuous?",
      "problem": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Determine where "
        },
        {
          "type": "math",
          "source": "f(x) = \\dfrac{x^2 - 1}{x - 1}"
        },
        { "type": "text", "text": " is continuous." }
      ],
      "solution": [
        [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "For "
          },
          { "type": "math", "source": "x \\neq 1" },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": " the function simplifies to "
          },
          { "type": "math", "source": "x + 1" },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": ", a polynomial, which is continuous everywhere it is defined."
          }
        ],
        [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "At "
          },
          { "type": "math", "source": "x = 1" },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": " the function is undefined — the first condition fails — so it is discontinuous there, even though "
          },
          {
            "type": "math",
            "source": "\\lim_{x \\to 1} f(x) = 2"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": " exists. The discontinuity is removable: redefining "
          },
          { "type": "math", "source": "f(1) = 2" },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": " would make the function continuous."
          }
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "callout",
      "kind": "important",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Continuity is three separate claims glued together. When a problem asks where a function is continuous, check each point against all three conditions — the most common error is stopping after the limit exists."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "source": "f \\text{ continuous at } a \\;\\iff\\; \\lim_{x \\to a} f(x) = f(a)",
      "display": true,
      "number": "(3)"
    },
    {
      "type": "formative",
      "prompt": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "A friend says: the limit of a function at "
        },
        { "type": "math", "source": "x = a" },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": " is 5, so the function must be continuous at "
        },
        { "type": "math", "source": "a" },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": ". Explain precisely why this does not follow."
        }
      ],
      "kind": "free_response"
    }
  ]
}